s | 48% | 66% | 48% | 64% |\n| 27 | Combined Ratio | 119% | 96% | 118% | 115% |\n| 28 | Investment income ratio | 2% | 2% | 2% | 7% |\n| 29 | Technical Reserves to net premium ratio | 268% | 158% | 253% | 60% |\n| 30 | Underwriting balance ratio | -23% | -1% | -21% | -18% |\n| 31 | Operating Profit Ratio | -16% | 14% | -15% | -9% |\n| 32 | Liquid Assets to liabilities ratio | 30% | 15% | 17% | 15% |\n| 33 | Net earning ratio | -13% | 4% | -12% | -10% |\n| 34 | Return on net worth ratio | -11% | 5% | -10% | -37% |\n| 35 | Available Solvency margin Ratio to Required Solvency Margin Ratio | 191% | 176% | 174% | 176% |\n| 36 | NPA Ratio |  |  |  |  |\n|  | Gross NPA Ratio | 0% | 0% | 0% | 0% |\n|  | Net NPA Ratio | 0% | 0% | 0% | 0% |\n\n## Notes\n\n1 Debt Equity ratio is calculated as Total borrowings divided by Equity. Networth defined by lRDAI is considered as Equity\n\n2 Debt service coverage ratio is calculated as Profit before interest and Tax divided by Interest expenses together with principal payment of long term debt during the period\n\n3 Interest service coverage ratio is calculated as Profit before interest and Tax divided by lnterest expenses of long term debt during the period\n\n4 Net worth represents shareholder's funds\n\n5 Not applicable to lnsurance Companies considering specific nature of the business\n\n6 Total debt to total assets is computed as borrowings divided by total assets\n\n7 The sector specific ratios have been calculated as per definition given in IRDAI analytical ratios disclosures\n\n---\n\n# ManipalCigna Health Insurance Company Limited\n\n# IRDA Registration No. 151 Dated November 13, 2013\n\n# CIN : U66000MH2012PLC227948\n\n# Registered Address- 4th Floor, Raheja Titanium, Off Express Highway, Goregaon East, Mumbai- 400063\n\n## Notes forming part of Financial Results\n\n1 The financial results have been prepared in accordance with the requirements of recognition and measurement principles under Section 133 of the Companies Act, 2013 read with relevant\nrules issued thereunder and other accounting principles accepted in India, to the extent applicable and are not inconsistent with the accounting principles as prescribed in the Insurance\nAct 1938 as amended (the \"Insurance Act\" ), the IRDAI Act 1999, IRDAI Regulations and orders/directions/circulars issued by the IRDAI and has been presented in accordance with the\npresentation & disclosure framework prescribed in Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) circular No. IRDAI/F&A/CIR/MISC/256/09/2021 dated September 30,\n2021 read with SEBI Master Circular No. SEBI/HO/DDHS/DDHS-PoD-1/P/CIR/2024/48 dated May 21, 2024 and the requirements of the Regulation 52 of the SEBI (Listing Obligations and\nDisclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015 (as amended) (\"Listing Regulations, 2015\"), to the extent applicable and the same were reviewed and recommended by the Audit committee on\nAugust 05, 2025 and then subsequently approved by the Board of Directors at their meeting held on August 06, 2025. The same has been subjected to limited review for the period ended\nJune 30, 2025 by the Statutory Auditors of the Company who have issued an unmodified opinion on the Financial Results\n\n2 The main business of the Company is Health Insurance. As such, there are no separate reportable segments as per the Accounting Standard (AS) - 17 'Segment Reporting' notified under\nsection 133 of the Companies Act 2013, read together with paragraph 7 of the Companies (Accounts) Rules 2016\n\n3 During the period ended June 30, 2025 the Company has allotted 4,88,70,005 and 5,08,64,700 equity shares at respective premiums of Rs 10.46 and Rs 9.66 each, with a face value of Rs 10\n\n4 Regulation 54(2) of the SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015 is not applicable as the Company has issued unsecured non-convertible debt securities.\nIn terms of Regulation 54(3) of SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, as amended from time to time, the Company has issued nonconvertible debentures, as a result, the requirement of security cover is not applicable on the Company\n\n5 In accordance with the IRDAI (Actuarial, Finance and Investment Functions of Insurers) Regulation, 2024 and Master circular thereon dated May 17, 2024 and subsequent clarification dated\nOctober 18, 2024, with effect from October 1, 2024 the Company has given the effect to recognise gross written premium on a 1/n basis where denotes the policy duration and\ncommission expenses paid and commission income accrued on such recorded gross written premium for applicable long-term products. This has resulted in a decrease in gross written\npremium by Rs.3,979 lakhs and net decrease in commission by Rs. 915 lakhs period ended June 30, 2025\n\n6 The Company has submitted application for forbearance in the limits of expense of management (EOM) over the permissible limits for the FY 2024-25 as per IRDAI (Expense of Management\nand Commission) Regulation 2024 (the EOM Regulation) with General Insurance Council (GIC). Excess of expense for the FY 2025-26 over the allowable limit prescribed in the EOM\n\n7 The Company has received show cause notice (SCN) dated June 15, 2023 from Directorate General of GST Intelligence (DGGI) demanding GST amount of Rs. 909 lakhs. The Authority has\ndisputed input tax credit (ITC) availed on certain services. The Company has deposited GST of Rs. 1,464 lakhs, out of which Rs. 1,357 lakhs has been paid under protest towards GST along\nwith applicable interest and penalty and the same has been disclosed as contingent liability as at June 30, 2025. Further to that, the Company has filed refund application for excess amount\nof GST deposited of Rs 107 lakhs against which the Company has subsequently received refund of Rs. 97 lakhs from the department while follow ups are going on for residual amount. The\nCompany has filed detailed reply with the department on February 09, 2024 and contesting the matter. Subsequently, Adjudicating Authority has issued an order on October 25, 2024\ncontinuing the demand against which the Company has filed an appeal with Appellate Authorities. Accordingly, the Company has disclosed the amount paid as under Schedule 12\n\n8 The Company has received final orders from the Income Tax department for AY 2016-17 and AY 2020-21 to AY 2022-23 reducing the tax losses by disallowing certain expenses while there is\nno tax demand. Appeals against these orders are filed before the appropriate Authorities. The management believes that the ultimate outcome of these proceedings will not have any\nmaterial adverse effect on the financial position and results of its operations and does not expect any disbursement on account of nil tax demand. With respect to penalty\nproceedings, it is not practicable to estimate the timing of cash outflows on account of pending resolution of the judicial / appellant proceedings.\n\n9 The Company has received show cause notice (SCN) dated May 01, 2025 from Directorate General of GST Intelligence (DGGI) demanding GST amount of Rs. 1,723 lakhs. The Authority has\ndisputed for not charging GST on policies issued to SEZ Units during the period of April 01, 2018 to September 30, 2023 treating them as Zero-Rated Supply. The Company is in the process of\nfiling the reply before the department and will contest the matter\n\n10 The Statement includes the financial results for the quarter ended March 31, 2025, being the balancing figures between the audited figures in respect of the full financial year ended March\n31, 2025 and published unaudited year-to-date figures up to December 31, 2024. The Financial results for the quarter ended June 30, 2024 is unaudited\n\n11 In view of the seasonality of Industry, the financial results for the quarters are not indicative of full year's expected performance\n\n12 Figures of previous year /quarters and year to date have been regrouped, wherever necessary, 